---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import IdempotencyReference from '../../components/IdempotencyReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Idempotent vs Non-Idempotent Operations Reference" description="Concrete examples of idempotent and non-idempotent API operations, and why it matters for retry safety.">
  <IdempotencyReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Idempotent vs Non-Idempotent: HTTP Methods at a Glance</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>GET</td><td>Yes</td><td>Read-only, never changes server state</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PUT</td><td>Yes</td><td>Replaces a resource with the exact same data every time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Yes</td><td>End state (resource gone) is the same no matter how many times it's called</td></tr>
      <tr><td>POST</td><td>No</td><td>Typically creates a new resource on each call</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PATCH</td><td>Depends</td><td>Idempotent if setting an absolute value; not if applying a relative change (e.g. incrementing)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>For any non-idempotent operation that genuinely needs safe retries (like payment creation), an <a href="/tools/idempotency-key-generator">idempotency key</a> lets the server recognize and deduplicate repeated requests.</p>

  <h2>Why this distinction changes how you write retry logic</h2>
  <p>Whether an operation is idempotent determines whether it's safe to blindly retry on a timeout or network error — retrying an idempotent operation has no additional effect beyond the first successful call, while retrying a non-idempotent one can cause duplicate side effects like double order creation.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check whether your specific operation type is idempotent by nature.</li>
    <li>For non-idempotent operations, use an idempotency key or other safeguard before implementing automatic retries.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Idempotency Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming PATCH is always non-idempotent</strong> — it depends on whether the update is absolute (idempotent) or relative like an increment (non-idempotent).</li>
    <li><strong>Blindly retrying POST requests</strong> on any error, without considering whether the original request may have actually succeeded server-side before the response was lost.</li>
    <li><strong>Not testing retry behavior explicitly</strong> — idempotency assumptions should be verified with actual tests, not just assumed from the HTTP method used.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When This Idempotency Reference Helps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing safe automatic retry logic in an HTTP client</li>
    <li>Reviewing an API's endpoints for idempotency guarantees</li>
    <li>Explaining to a team why a specific endpoint needs an idempotency key</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Idempotency FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is the difference between idempotent and non-idempotent?</strong> An idempotent operation produces the same end result no matter how many times it's repeated. A non-idempotent operation can produce a different result — or an additional side effect, like creating a duplicate record — with each repeated call.</p>
  <p><strong>Is DELETE always idempotent?</strong> Yes by convention — deleting an already-deleted resource should still result in it being gone, which is the same end state regardless of how many times it's called.</p>
  <p><strong>Is POST ever idempotent?</strong> Not by default, but it can be made idempotent using an idempotency key — the server recognizes a repeated request with the same key and returns the original result instead of creating a duplicate.</p>
</ToolLayout>
